# PostgreSQL 16 — `pg_hba.conf` (Ubuntu)

Canonical rules from **`/etc/postgresql/16/main/pg_hba.conf`** on the Ubuntu app/DB host (`192.168.222.202` / xbox2).

After edits: `sudo systemctl reload postgresql@16-main` (or equivalent). Order matters — first matching line wins.

**Local admin:** `local … postgres peer` allows `sudo -u postgres psql` on the unix socket only. The `host … postgres … reject` lines still block **TCP** login as `postgres` from any IP. App access uses **`test_user1`**.

```conf
local   all             postgres        peer
local   all             test_user1      peer
host    all             test_user1      127.0.0.1/32    scram-sha-256
host    all             test_user1      ::1/128         scram-sha-256

# REJECT postgres from ANY TCP connection (local or remote) — does not affect unix socket
host    all             postgres        0.0.0.0/0               reject
host    all             postgres        ::/0                    reject

